    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d483">

                <pb facs="0420" n="410" sameAs="frus:frus1949v08/pg410"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.001 Chiang Kai-shek/7–849: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Taipei</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Edgar</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Taipei</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-07-08T15:00:00+09:00">July 8, 1949—3 p.
                            m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 8—7:51 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>274. Chen Cheng<anchor n="54" xml:id="d483-f1"/> yesterday told Steele, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Herald Tribune</hi>, [that] Generalissimo<anchor n="55"
                        xml:id="d483-f2"/> will go Canton next week to develop solidarity on basis
                    recent manifesto. May go inland after Canton. Chen stated past defeats not due
                    lack armies or matériel but lack will to fight. Foresees resurgence national
                    spirit through Generalissimo’s efforts. Sent Canton 117, repeated Secretary of
                    State 274.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Edgar</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d483-f1" n="54" xml:id="d483-f1-content">
                <p>Governor of Taiwan (Formosa).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d483-f2" n="55" xml:id="d483-f2-content">
                <p>Chiang Kai-shek, who retired as President of the Republic of China on January 21,
                    1949.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
